3.9.6.m:
Now these Āprī verses:
Prajāpati, having created creatures, thought himself emptied out.
He saw these Āprī verses;
with them he pleased himself.
The sacrifice is indeed Prajāpati.
When there are these Āprī verses,
with them the sacrificer pleases the sacrifice itself.
By means of the omentum of the victim the gods went to the heavenly world at the morning pressing.
When they proceed with the omentum at the morning pressing,
by means of the omentum of the victim the sacrificer goes to the heavenly world at the morning pressing.
Going to heaven, they shook off their bones and bodies.
When they proceed with the limbs at the third pressing,
[it is] for the ransom of the bones and bodies.
They bathe the victim;
whatever impurity and unfitness for sacrifice has settled upon it, they cleanse that.
Having brought up the victim, they churn the fire,
for the securing of cattle.
There are indeed two thunderbolts:
one terrible,
the other auspicious.
That which is dry is terrible;
that which is moist is auspicious.
By the thunderbolt indeed heroic deed is performed;
he thus takes up the thunderbolt
for heroic deed.
"For refreshment, thee!" [he says];
for refreshment [he designates] it.
"Thou art a protector," [he says];
for it protects.
"The divine clans have approached the gods," [he says];
cattle indeed are these divine clans.
"The conveyers, the eager ones," [he says];
the officiating priests and the dhiṣṇya hearths are indeed these conveyers, the eager ones.
"O Bṛhaspati, hold fast the treasures," [he says];
Bṛhaspati is indeed the bráhman;
by means of the bráhman he holds it.
"May they sweeten the oblations for thee," [he says];
made sweetened he seizes it.
"O divine Tvaṣṭṛ, delight in wealth," [he says];
for Tvaṣṭṛ transforms shapes.
"Rich in cattle, the unoffered," [he says];
cattle are indeed the rich ones;
he holds it.
"At the impulse of the god Savitṛ," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ he takes it up.
"With the arms of the Aśvins," [he says];
the two Aśvins are indeed the two Adhvaryus of the gods.
"With the hands of Pūṣan," [he says];
by means of the deities indeed [he takes it up].
There are indeed two nooses:
one terrible,
the other auspicious.
That which is sacrificial is terrible;
that which is non-sacrificial is auspicious.
"With the noose of divine sacrificial oblation of Truth," [he says];
with whichever noose is sacrificial, with that he seizes it.
"I fasten [thee]," [he says];
for men fasten [it].
"Pleasing to such-and-such [deity], thee!" [he says];
for whichever deity the victim is seized, pleasing to that [deity] he makes it.
"Bold men," [he says];
for men hold fast.
Dry indeed is this [post], unfit for sacrifice.
When he binds the victim,
he makes both of them fit for sacrifice.
He binds [it] facing westward;
for facing westward cattle approach the sacrifice.
In its vital breaths the deities are present.
When he binds the victim,
he binds the deities facing westward for him.
"From the waters, from the plants, thee!" [he says];
for it [comes] from the waters;
"Pleasing to the plants, I sprinkle thee," [he says];
for whichever deity the victim is seized, pleasing to that [deity] he makes it.
"May thy mother approve thee, may thy father [approve thee]," [he says];
approved by mother, father, brother, and friend, he seizes it.
"Bring the approving gods to the god-seeking sacrificer," [he says];
to the deities he assigns it.
"Thou art the swell of the waters," [he says];
for it is the swell of the waters.
"A well-tasted oblation being, may the divine waters sweeten [it]," [he says];
made sweetened he seizes it.
Then, when he holds [water] underneath and sprinkles upon [it],
he endows it with sacrificial essence on all sides.
"May thy wind unite with the wind," [he says];
he causes its breath to follow the wind.
"Thy limbs with the adorable ones," [he says];
he has made it sacrificial.
"The lord of sacrifice with the prayer," [he says];
he endows the sacrificer with the prayer of the sacrifice.
The Āghāra is the head;
the oblation is the body.
When he anoints the Āghāra that is to be sprinkled with the victim,
he places the head of the sacrifice on its body.
The chip is indeed a thunderbolt;
the knife is a thunderbolt.
When he anoints with the knife,
he lays it low with the thunderbolt itself.
With that [blade] which has been anointed, he cuts off a portion of the cooked [victim];
for this has become an oblation;
with an oblation he cuts off [a portion] of the oblation.

3.10.3.m:
He cuts off from the heart;
he cuts off by that from the mind.
Moreover, from whatever extent the animal victim is, from that it is cut off.
He cuts off from the tongue;
he cuts off by that from speech.
Moreover, with this [part] alone he eats the sap from the tips of the plants.
He cuts off from the breast-piece;
he cuts off by that from the chest.
Moreover, here that sap is established.
Having cut off from the foreleg, he cuts off from the two flanks,
for the embracing of the sacrifice.
He cuts off from the liver;
he cuts off by that from the middle.
He cuts off from the two kidneys;
he cuts off by that from form.
Having cut off from the rump, he cuts off from the rectum;
he cuts off that last;
for the life-breath is last.
If he were to cut off from another, subsequent [limb], he would close up his breath;
he would be prone to die.
Therefore he cuts off from that last;
for the life-breath is last.
Having cut off from the heart, he cuts off from the tongue;
he thus approaches the animal victim in regular order.
Moreover, so it is cut off from this whole animal victim.
He cuts off eleven times.
Ten indeed are the breaths of the animal victim;
the self is the eleventh;
thus it is cut off from this whole animal victim.
Eleven indeed are these cut-off portions.
He cuts them off twice each;
that makes twenty-two.
Three are the triple limbs;
that makes twenty-five.
By the twenty-fivefold stoma Manu created offspring;
this is indeed the Manu-stoma,
for procreation.
This indeed is cut off from the whole animal victim;
now, of the out-breath, in-breath, and diffused breath, from these it is not cut off.
When he cuts off from the triple limbs,
by that alone it is cut off from their out-breath, in-breath, and diffused breath.
Viśvarūpa, son of Tvaṣṭṛ, vomited upon the cattle.
Therefore cattle have all forms.
The animal victim vomited upon became putrid;
therefore the marrow of the ribs is not to be eaten.
It did not reach below;
that indeed is living.
Therefore he cuts off from here.
He cuts off from the limbs diagonally;
he thereby makes the animal victim lame.
Therefore cattle go moving their limbs diagonally.
The whole animal victim belongs to Agni;
and yet it is seized for another deity.
When he cuts off for Agni from the foreleg in the front part, from the rectum in the middle, [and] from the rump in the rear part,
thus it is cut off for Agni from this whole animal victim.
He makes the rectum threefold;
for the animal victim is threefold at that time.
The triple limbs and the cut-off portions are placed together;
as much as the animal victim is, in that he places life.
He makes the fat threefold;
for cattle have the form of fat.
All the limbs of a fattening animal become fat;
as much as the animal victim is, in that he places the form of fat.
“The sacrifices of men are full of corpses,” said Yajñavacas Rājastambāyana;
“men drive the animal victim away from here,
[yet] they do not go yonder.”
When he sacrifices having placed gold [under it],
with gold as its light he causes him to go to the heavenly world.

3.10.4.m:
He stirs the fat-oblation;
he thereby stirs the sap.
He stirs it with the axe;
for with this he cuts off the other limbs;
thereby he makes it a cut-off portion.
Moreover, by the thunderbolt he secures the oblation.
With soup he appeases whatever follows it;
sap and nourishment, indeed, is the soup;
with nourishment and sap he makes the animal victim prosper.
Moreover, he unites into it as much as the animal victim is.
He covers it with the flank,
for the enclosing of nourishment.
Killed, indeed, dead, gone to yonder world is this [victim].
When he touches the animal victim,
he causes it to come into being again.
They say: 'Having first caused the offering to reach the deities, then the body should be caused to mount [after it].'
When, having offered the oblations, he touches the animal victim,
he indeed, having first caused the offering to reach the deities, then causes the body to mount [after it].
He offers at the half-verse;
this [earth], indeed, is the half-verse;
yonder [sky] is the half-verse;
between these two is the atmosphere;
these creatures are the atmosphere;
he places the sap in the creatures alone.
He sacrifices to Vanaspati;
Vanaspati is Soma;
these creatures belong to Soma;
he places the sap in the creatures alone.
In-breath and out-breath, indeed, are the speckled butter;
the body is the oblation.
When he offers the speckled butter,
he places in-breath and out-breath in the body of the animal victim.
He offers to the quarters;
he moistens these quarters with sap.
He offers the last to the east;
they turn back toward the east.
They bring the part above the udder to the Hotṛ;
the Hotṛ belongs to the All-gods;
these creatures belong to the All-gods.
Creatures, indeed, live along upon the udder;
he makes them all rich in milk.
He performs the subsidiary sacrifice with the rectum;
the rectum, indeed, is the vital breaths;
from the vital breath creatures are born,
for procreation.
He performs the subsidiary sacrifice from the thick end;
for creatures are born from the thick end.
If he were to perform the subsidiary sacrifice from the thin end, he would obstruct procreation;
therefore he performs the subsidiary sacrifice from the thick end.
Without severing he performs the subsidiary sacrifice,
for the non-severing of the vital breaths.
If he were to sever, he would sever the vital breaths.
If he should hate someone, he should sever his [rectum];
he severs his vital breaths.
[Some say:] 'It is not to be offered in subsidiary sacrifice;
if he were to offer it in subsidiary sacrifice, he would offer the vital breaths in subsidiary sacrifice; he would be prone to die.'
Regarding this they say:
'It is indeed to be offered in subsidiary sacrifice;
the subsidiary sacrifice is procreation;
even he is procreated who offers in subsidiary sacrifice.'
'O Ābhāradvāja,' Śucivṛkṣa Gaupālāyana used to say,
'how then were there creatures when the sacrifice was not accompanied by subsidiary sacrifices?'
Creatures were not procreated;
plants did not bear fruit.
When indeed the sacrifice was joined with subsidiary sacrifices, then creatures were procreated,
then plants bore fruit.
Eleven are the fore-offerings;
eleven are the after-offerings;
eleven are the subsidiary sacrifices;
that makes thirty-three.
Thirty-three are the deities;
they are sought after and pleased by these [offerings] for him.
Then this animal victim of his has been sacrificed to not without reaching, not without attainment.
Whatever is its measure, to that he causes it to go.
The entire animal victim is heavenly;
if he were to offer elsewhere than in the Āhavanīya, it would not be heavenly.
They bring the fire from the animal-cooking [fire];
thereby the entire animal victim is heavenly.
From where he cut off the former [portion], from there he cuts off the next;
therefore from the same womb animal victims of various forms are born.
The sacrifice indeed overflowed the gods;
the gods dammed it up with the subsidiary sacrifices, so as not to overflow.
When there are these subsidiary sacrifices,
with them they dam up the sacrifice,
so as not to overflow.
Just as a man has an aperture behind, so the sacrifice has an aperture behind;
the gods stopped it up with the subsidiary sacrifices, that it might be without apertures.
When there are these subsidiary sacrifices,
with them he stops up the sacrifice,
that it might be without apertures.

3.10.6.m:
The gods and the Asuras contended.
Their faculties and heroic powers departed.
Ṛc and Sāman departed from them then,
[as did] cattle, speech, faculty, [and] the in-breath and out-breath.
With them Indra desired, "May I attain union."
"Let Indra possessing bay steeds eat the roasted barley!" [he says];
Ṛc and Sāman are Indra's two bay steeds;
with Ṛc and Sāman he attained union.
"Possessing Pūṣan, the porridge!" [he says];
Pūṣan is cattle;
with cattle he attained union.
"Possessing Sarasvatī, possessing Bhāratī, the puffed rice!" [he says];
Sarasvatī is speech;
with speech he attained union.
"The flatbread of Indra!" [he says];
Indra is faculty;
with faculty he attained union.
"The curd-mixture of Mitra and Varuṇa!" [he says];
Mitra and Varuṇa are the in-breath and out-breath;
with the in-breath and out-breath he attained union.
Then the gods prospered,
the Asuras were defeated.
So he who knows thus attains union with these very faculties, heroic powers, and these greatnesses;
he prospers in himself;
his rival is defeated.
Dīrghajihvī licked the morning pressing of the gods.
It became fermented.
It became the curd-mixture.
Therefore the curd-mixture has as it were the appearance of being fermented.
It should belong to Maitrāvaruṇa at the morning pressing.
Mitra and Varuṇa are the in-breath and out-breath;
the in-breath and out-breath are thereby placed in the mouth of the sacrifice.
The rectum is not to be turned around.
If he were to turn the rectum around, an upward-swelling would kill the offspring.
[It is done] so that the demons do not overpower [the sacrifice].
The sacrificial post is erected in front.
If they were to carry the animal victim in front of the sacrificial post, then the demons would overpower the sacrifice.
It is to be carried between the fire and the sacrificial post,
so that the demons do not overpower [the sacrifice].

3.10.7.m:
"Go to the ocean, svāhā!" he says;
he thereby sacrifices to the place of waters.
"Go to the atmosphere, svāhā!" [he says];
he thereby deposits semen.
"Go to divine Savitṛ, svāhā!" he says,
for procreation indeed.
"Go to day and night, svāhā!" he says;
for after day and night offspring are born.
"Go to Mitra and Varuṇa, svāhā!" he says;
Mitra and Varuṇa are the in-breath and out-breath;
he deposits in-breath and out-breath in them.
"Go to Heaven and Earth, svāhā!" he says;
by these two he encloses them after they have been created.
"Go to the metres, svāhā!" he says;
the metres are speech;
he deposits speech in them.
"Go to Soma, svāhā!" he says;
Soma is food;
he deposits food in them.
"Go to the sacrifice, svāhā!" he says;
he makes them worthy of sacrifice.
"Go to the heavenly cloud, svāhā!" he says;
he pours down rain for them.
"Go to Agni Vaiśvānara, svāhā!" he says;
Agni Vaiśvānara is the year;
he establishes them in the year;
he makes them possess the lifespan of a year.
"Hold back mind, heart!" he says,
for the protection of the vital breaths.
"To the plants, thee!" he says;
he deposits sap in the plants.
Whoever, whether knowing or unknowing, offers subsidiary sacrifices making pairs, he pairs these vital breaths.
When he says:
"Hold back mind, heart!",
he thereby arranges the vital breaths according to the seasons.
The animal victim, when seized, grieves;
its sorrow comes together in the middle;
it enters the heart.
If it should touch a man, sorrow would reach humans;
if it should touch a cow, sorrow would reach cattle;
if it should touch wood, sorrow would reach trees;
if it should touch grass, sorrow would reach plants;
if it should touch water, sorrow would reach the waters.
What is moist and without water, that is appeased;
into that it should be cast down;
thereby he averts sorrow from the deity.
Thus ends the tenth prapāṭhaka named Pātnīvanta in the third kāṇḍa.

3.11.6.m:
King Soma, immortal, pressed, the sap of plants, of the waters;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
From the waters the curlew, the Āṅgirasa, drank off [the milk] through thought;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
From the waters the swan, dwelling in the clear, drank off Soma with the meters;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
From food, from the flowing [liquor], the nobility drank off the sap with the sacred word;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
Semen abandons urine as the faculty enters the womb;
the embryo, covered with the chorion, abandons the amnion through birth;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
Having seen the two forms, Prajāpati distinguished truth and untruth;
Prajāpati placed lack of faith in untruth, faith in truth;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
Through the Veda Prajāpati drank off the two forms, the pressed and the unpressed;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.
Having seen the sap of the flowing [liquor], Prajāpati drank off the bright with the bright,
the milk, the Soma;
by truth [is] real faculty, the special drinking, the bright [juice] of the stalk;
this faculty of Indra, milk, immortal honey.

3.11.8.m:
At the impulse of god Savitar, with the arms of the two Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I take you.
Three times eleven are the thirty-three gods, rich in gifts,
having Bṛhaspati as purohita; at the impulse of god Savitar
let the gods protect you with the gods!
Let the first ones anoint you with the second ones;
the second ones you with the third ones;
the third ones you with truth;
truth you with the sacred word;
the sacred word you with the yajus-formulas;
the yajus-formulas you with the chants;
the chants you with the verses;
the verses you with the invitatory formulas;
the invitatory formulas you with the offering verses;
the offering verses you with the vaṣaṭ-calls;
let the vaṣaṭ-calls anoint you with the oblations!
Let the oblations fulfill your desires, O so-and-so!
With the brilliance of the two Aśvins I anoint you for spiritual splendour!
With the heroic power of Sarasvatī I anoint you for fame, for edible food!
With the faculty of Indra I anoint you for vigour, for strength!
Bhūḥ, svāhā!
My head is fortune, my face is fame, my hair and beard are flash [of glory];
my breath is the king, immortal, my sight the emperor, my hearing the sovereign.
My tongue is auspicious, my speech great, my mind fury, my wrath self-ruler;
joys and supreme pleasures are my fingers, limbs, Mitra my overwhelming might.
My arms are strength, faculty; my two hands are action, heroic power;
my torso is nobility, my chest [dominion].
My ribs are the realm, my belly, shoulders, neck, and hips;
my two thighs, two cubits, two knees are the clans, my limbs on all sides.
My navel is intellect, discernment; my anus respect, my pudenda [dignity];
my two testicles are joys and delights, my penis is Bhaga, good fortune.
My bodily hairs are forward effort, my skin compliance, approach;
my flesh submission, wealth my bone, my marrow compliance.
With two shanks, with two feet I am steadfast, an established king among the clan;
I am established in the priesthood, I am established in the nobility;
I am established among horses, I am established among cattle;
I am established in offspring, I am established on the back [of the sky];
I am established in the breaths, I am established in the self;
I am established in heaven and earth, in the sacrifice!
